# SPPARKS additive_temperature_texture: multiple identical Rosenthal
# scans along the x-direction over a single-crystal substrate.
#
# Purpose: study cumulative thermal effects of repeated passes -- heat
# accumulation in the substrate, melt-pool overlap with re-solidified
# tracks, and grain selection in the consolidating powder layer.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Half-space (free-surface) Rosenthal convention
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# SPPARKS implements the textbook image-source form: the laser plane
# (= scan_layer_z) is treated as a thermally insulating free surface.
# Sites ABOVE the laser plane (z_rel > 0) are vacuum and stay at T0;
# sites BELOW the laser plane (z_rel <= 0) get the textbook half-space
# kernel with the explicit image factor of 2:
#
#     T = T0 + 2 * lambda*Q / (2*pi*k*R) * exp(-v*(xi+R)/(2*alpha))
#
# In this example the laser plane is set at the TOP of the box, so
# every lattice site is below the free surface and the cutoff is
# invisible -- the entire box is part of the half-space.
#
# Pauza paper note: the paper uses the kernel with prefactor 1/(2*pi)
# and absorbs the image factor of 2 into lambda. To match the same
# physical pool size, lambda values used here are HALF the published
# paper values (e.g. paper case-3 lambda=0.155 -> 0.0775 here).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Characteristic scales and box sizing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Effective Rosenthal melt-pool half-width (centerline, top surface):
#
#     R_m  =  2 * lambda * Q / (2*pi * k * (T_m - T0))
#
# For the parameters used below (Q=240 W, lambda=0.0775, k=11.2,
# T_m-T0 = 1000 K) this gives R_m ~= 0.53 mm. The factor of 2 in
# R_m is the image source.
#
# Box (in physical units):
#     x: 2.00 mm  ~ 3.8 * R_m  (path is 1.5 mm = ~2.8 * R_m)
#     y: 0.40 mm  ~ 0.8 * R_m  (pool clips at the y walls)
#     z: 0.30 mm  ~ 0.6 * R_m  (pool clips at the z bottom; the box
#                                 acts as a thin slab, not a far-field
#                                 half-space)
#
# Lattice (sc/26n) at dx = 5 um:  400 x 80 x 60 = 1.92 M sites
# Pool half-width is resolved at R_m / dx ~= 106 cells.
#
# Note on box vs pool: the y and z extents are smaller than R_m, so
# the analytical Rosenthal field extends past the box walls. The
# 'boundary n n n' setting means those external sites simply don't
# exist; sites at the walls melt normally. This is the intended
# regime for the multi-scan study -- a thin slab of powder + substrate
# rather than a far-field semi-infinite half-space. Reduce Q if you
# want a fully enclosed pool (Q ~ 45 W gives R_m ~ 0.2 mm, which just
# fits inside the y half-extent and the substrate depth).
#
# Note on rescaling: the Rosenthal solution is geometrically scale-free
# in the combination R/R_m, so increasing Q by a factor of f and the
# box dimensions and dx by the same factor reproduces the identical
# normalized field. The paper J G Pauza et al 2021 Modelling Simul.
# Mater. Sci. Eng. 29 055019, Table B1 case 3 uses Q=285 W with
# dx=1 um; matching that resolution at full Q on this box ratio would
# be ~13 G sites and is HPC-scale, not example-scale. Bump dx down
# and Q up together for a higher-resolution study.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Substrate / powder layout (lattice indices; dx = 5 um)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   z in [ 0, 40)  : single-crystal substrate  (0.20 mm of bulk)
#   z in [40, 60]  : powder layer              (0.10 mm of fresh powder)
#   laser plane    : z = 60 (= 0.30 mm)        at the top of the powder
#                    (and at the top of the simulation box)
#
# This is the textbook LPBF geometry: substrate at the bottom, powder
# layer on top, laser scanning the top surface of the powder.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scan path
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   1.5 mm linear path in +x along the centerline (y = 40, z = 60),
#   from x = 0.25 mm to x = 1.75 mm, repeated NSCANS times.
#   Path duration per repeat = 1.5e-3 m / 1.2 m/s = 1.25 ms.
#   Total scan time = NSCANS * 1.25 ms.

variable NSCANS equal 5

# app_style additive_temperature_texture <nspins> <dx[m]> <dt[s]> <nrefine>
app_style additive_temperature_texture 1000000 5e-6 5e-6 2

shell mkdir -p DumpFiles_rosenthal_multiscan

seed             56789

dimension        3
lattice          sc/26n 1.0
region           box block 0 400 0 80 0 60

boundary         n n n

create_box       box
create_sites     box

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Initial site state
#
#   1. Random grain ids (powder default) over the whole box.
#   2. active_flag (i2) = 1 everywhere (active layer).
#   3. Substrate region (z < 40 in lattice units, i.e. z < 0.20 mm):
#      overwrite with a single grain id and identity quaternion, and
#      set active_flag = 3 (consolidated solid).
#
# Multiple 'if' clauses on the same set command are AND'd, but a
# single 'if z < 40' is sufficient for an axis-aligned slab cut.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

set site range 1 1000000
set i2 value 1

# Single-crystal substrate. d1..d4 = (q0, qx, qy, qz) = identity.
set i1 value 42      #if z < 40
set i2 value 3       #if z < 40
set d1 value 1.0     #if z < 40
set d2 value 0.0     #if z < 40
set d3 value 0.0     #if z < 40
set d4 value 0.0     #if z < 40

sector           yes
sweep            random mask no

diag_style       array d6 sum

temperature      0.0
misorientation_target gradient

# AM-specific (Table B1 IN718-like)
liquidus         1573
solidus          1523
solid_front_vel  4 -1.0302e-7 1.0533e-4 2.2196e-3 0
nucleation_density      1e14
critical_undercooling   5
undercooling_deviation  3
mean_nuclei_volume      8.4375e-16
nuclei_volume_deviation 1.5625e-17

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rosenthal source: standard mode (Eq. B4, half-space)
# args: <Q[W]> <lambda> <k[W/(mK)]> <alpha[m^2/s]> <T0[K]>
#
# lambda=0.0775 here = paper case-3 lambda=0.155 / 2 (image factor of
# 2 is now explicit in rosenthal_pointwise).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
setup_temperature_source rosenthal standard 240.0 0.0775 11.2 2.0959e-6 573.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multi-scan path. Coordinates in meters; Z is the laser plane.
# 1.5 mm linear scan at 1.2 m/s, repeated ${NSCANS} times.
# Laser plane at z = 0.30 mm = lattice index 60 = top of the powder.
# Path centered transversely at y = 0.20 mm = lattice index 40.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
rosenthal_path start 0.25e-3 0.20e-3 0.30e-3 end 1.75e-3 0.20e-3 speed 1.2 repeats ${NSCANS}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stochastic ΔW/W, ΔD/D modulation of the (anisotropic) Rosenthal pool.
#
# In-source PSD streaming generator (no Python preprocessing): SPPARKS
# synthesizes a Lorentzian-correlated noise chain on the fly using an
# AR(1) filter (alpha = exp(-dx/tau)) driven by bivariate Gaussian
# innovations that enforce the W/D Pearson correlation rho.
#
# Because the cumulative laser arc length advances monotonically across
# the ${NSCANS} repeats and the AR(1) chain is open-ended, each pass
# naturally consumes a statistically independent 1.5 mm window of the
# same Lorentzian process (tau=200 um << 1.5 mm). The same seed
# produces an identical chain on every MPI rank.
#
# The command auto-promotes the source from STANDARD to ANISOTROPIC
# (eta_y=eta_z=1).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
rosenthal_fluctuations psd lorentzian sigma_W 0.05 sigma_D 0.06 tau 200e-6 rho 0.5 seed 12345 dx 5e-6

shell cd DumpFiles_rosenthal_multiscan

stats            5e-4

# Dump T (d6 = darray[5]), grain id, active_flag, and the full
# quaternion (d1..d4) every 0.5 ms. NSCANS=5 passes take 6.25 ms;
# at 0.5 ms cadence we get ~12 in-scan snapshots plus cool-down frames.
dump             1 text 5e-4 dump.multiscan.* id i1 i2 d1 d2 d3 d4 d6 x y z
dump_modify      1 thresh i2 > 1

# Run for slightly longer than NSCANS * 1.25 ms = 6.25 ms to capture
# post-scan cooling and the final solidified microstructure.
run              15e-3
shell cd ../
